Actually, let me look at the `ares__parse_into_addrinfo2` function more carefully. There's a potential issue with how it handles the CNAME:

```c
if (rr_class == C_IN && rr_type == T_CNAME)
{
    got_cname = 1;
    status = ares__expand_name_for_response(aptr, abuf, alen, &rr_data, &len);
    if (status != ARES_SUCCESS)
    {
        goto failed_stat;
    }

    /* Decode the RR data and replace the hostname with it. */
    hostname = rr_data;

    cname = ares__append_addrinfo_cname(&cnames);
    if (!cname)
    {
        status = ARES_ENOMEM;
        ares_free(rr_data);
        goto failed_stat;
    }
    cname->ttl = rr_ttl;
    cname->alias = rr_name;
    cname->name = rr_data;
}
```

After this, `rr_name` is NOT freed (it's assigned to `cname->alias`). And `rr_data` is assigned to both `hostname` and `cname->name`. Then the loop continues to the next iteration. But `rr_name` is now owned by `cname`, so it shouldn't be freed. And `rr_data` is now owned by both `hostname` and `cname->name` - wait, that's a problem! Both `hostname` and `cname->name` point to the same allocation. When the function cleans up, `cname->name` will be freed by `ares__freeaddrinfo_cnames`, and `hostname` is a local variable that's not separately freed. But `hostname` is used for comparison with subsequent RR names. This is fine as long as `cname->name` is not freed before the comparison.

Actually, looking at the cleanup code:
```c
if (status == ARES_SUCCESS)
{
    ares__addrinfo_cat_nodes(&ai->nodes, nodes);
    if (got_cname)
    {
        ares__addrinfo_cat_cnames(&ai->cnames, cnames);
        return status;
    }
    ...
}

return status;

failed_stat:
    ares_free(rr_name);
    ares__freeaddrinfo_cnames(cnames);
    ares__freeaddrinfo_nodes(nodes);
    return status;
```

On success with CNAME, `cnames` is attached to `ai->cnames`. On failure, `cnames` is freed. This seems correct.

